  a) Wall cap check granularity: MON_S poll interval — fine.

  b) kill_session: `os.killpg(pid, SIGKILL)` — killpg with the process group; start_new_session makes pgid == pid. TIME (GNU time) is the direct child; diaWeaver is the grandchild in same session/group → killed too. But diaWeaver could itself fork setsid'd grandchildren (OpenMS doesn't typically). Also TIME might... OK.

  c) The memory floor: `m["memavail_kb"] < MEM_FLOOR * m["memtotal_kb"]` — MEM_FLOOR presumably 0.05. On a 224-core shared node with dt's -Xmx400G... the pair arms (dt, spx) run via run_logged WITHOUT the memory floor — only cells. But dt with 400G heap could push MemAvailable under floor and trigger a kill of... no, run_capped only monitors its own cell process. But a CELL could be killed because the REFERENCE arms... no, cells run after the pair, sequentially. But OTHER tenants on the shared node could push MemAvailable low → cell killed at hour 11 → group fails → re-run whole group (26h lost). The guard aborts instead of retrying/waiting. That's by design (G-V16 aborts), but on a shared 224-core node, MemAvailable under 5% due to a neighbor is plausible → kills the run. It's "fail safe" though — produces refusal not wrong number. Design intent. Note it.

  d) "Is the stopped check ordered correctly relative to parse_time_log/check_exit?" In run_cell:

  ```python
  rc, stopped = run_capped(...)
  rec.update(end=..., time_rc=rc, stopped=stopped, ...)
  check_identity(...)
  if stopped:
      die("G-V16", ...)
  rec.update(parse_time_log(tl, "G-V16"), cpu=time_cpu(tl))
  check_exit(rec, rc, "G-V16", name)
  ```

  Stopped checked first — good, because a killed process yields garbage time log (GNU time on SIGKILL of its child: time writes exit status 137? Actually GNU time reports "Command terminated by signal 9" and exit status 0? The FAKE_TIME notes "Command terminated by signal 7 comes with Exit status: 0". So check_exit would catch signal... but parse_time_log would succeed if time wrote its lines. Order fine: stopped → die before parse. But subtle: if the cap fires at the exact moment the tool finished successfully — p.poll() None, cap exceeded, kill session of an exiting process → killpg may hit ProcessLookupError (handled) or kill the tool mid-write → output truncated → subsequent checks catch. Marginal race: tool finished, wrote everything, exited; runner kills session between exit and poll — killpg ProcessLookupError → pass. Then `p.wait()` → returncode 0, but why is set → die "killed: wall cap" even though the tool completed fine. Slight over-abort, fails safe. Fine.

  e) Can diaWeaver outlive the kill? killpg SIGKILL — no. Unless diaWeaver spawned a NEW session (setsid) — e.g., OpenMS doesn't. TIME's own process group — start_new_session on `time`, children inherit. OK.

  f) Runner's SIGTERM: `except BaseException: kill_session; p.wait(); raise` — KeyboardInterrupt/SystemExit → killed. Good. But SIGTERM default handler terminates the process WITHOUT raising... unless the runner installs a SIGTERM handler raising SystemExit (the pair doc mentions "SIGTERM (sys.exit)"). Assuming so. If the runner gets SIGKILL, the cell session survives → diaWeaver keeps running; on relaunch, busy_patterns includes diaWeaver pattern → load_gate waits 20 min then proceeds "contended"! The busy gate doesn't abort after wait — it records contended and proceeds. So an orphaned diaWeaver from a SIGKILLed runner would run CONCURRENTLY with the re-run — both reading the same staged... no, staged copy deleted? remove_tree happens in finally of run_group — but if runner SIGKILLed, no finally → staged copy lingers, orphan keeps running, new launch stages a fresh copy, orphan contends CPU → contention measured by ArmMonitor, flagged. Numbers plausible but flagged. Also busy_patterns detection: orphan's argv contains "diaWeaver -in" → busy → gate waits load_wait_min=20min then proceeds anyway with contended flag. Hmm, G-V06 proceeds after wait — design. Not a wrong number per se (flagged). Note it.

  g) proc_snapshot(): if /proc unreadable, m=None → memory check skipped silently (m and ...). Preflight requires /proc readable. OK.

  h) MON_S sleep via p.wait(timeout=MON_S) — efficient. But note: between iterations, `m = proc_snapshot()` — called every MON_S. Fine.

  i) `run_capped` on cap: breaks, then `p.wait()` (no timeout) — SIGKILL → dies promptly. OK.

  j) What if MEM_FLOOR check fires due to the cell itself legitimately using memory: design.

  **Question 5: run_group exception handling** — covered above: non-Abort exceptions in a cell (ValueError from mzml_scan float(), zlib.error, binascii.Error, struct errors) propagate past the per-cell `except Abort` to the outer `except Exception` → whole group "CRASH", remaining cells unrun. Contradicts stated semantics "a failed cell does not stop the next one". Concrete input: a truncated pseudo_spectra.mzML where the m/z binary base64 decodes to bytes whose length isn't a multiple of 8 → `mz.frombytes` ValueError during G-V17 scan → group CRASH after dwv_default; dwv_pickim never runs; group record failed → full 26h re-run. Severity: should-fix (would bite only on malformed output — but malformed output is exactly what G-V17 exists for; G-V17 should catch it as a clean cell failure). Wait — verify_mzml catches unterminated output first. A well-terminated but weird file (e.g. valueless defaultArrayLength... `int(m.group(1))` fine). `float(prec[k])` — diaWeaver writes "0" etc. zlib.error: raw output has numpress arrays; mzml_scan(src) with decode=False doesn't decode! Only dst. dst comes from FileFilter. If FileFilter writes corrupt zlib → zlib.error → CRASH. Theoretical-ish. Also `statistics.median` fine.

  Also: BaseException (KeyboardInterrupt) inside a cell → outer BaseException handler → group KILLED, staged deleted, record written, re-raised → queue stops. For SIGTERM that's intended.

  Another Q5 sub-point: "what happens to the reference arms' results" — they're in rec["arms"], record status failed/CRASH → pair_done false → next launch re-runs whole group including the reference pair. Expensive but safe.

  **Question 6: no-op digest.** Covered: content digest order-independent; can two runs of same arm differ? The reencoded file's block content: diaWeaver writes float data arrays (fragment_trace_id, fragment_window_id) — if trace ids are assigned in thread-completion order, they may differ run to run → but G-V18 compares dwv_default vs dwv_pickim in the SAME run — if the flag is a no-op, outputs should be identical only if diaWeaver is deterministic. If nondeterministic, a genuine no-op may show content differ → NOT flagged → the no-op is silently accepted as "differs". The selftest only tests the equal case with a deterministic fake. The reverse (false flag when arms genuinely differ but collide) is cryptographically implausible. The interesting failure: real no-op NOT flagged because of run-to-run nondeterminism — but that's a true positive miss, and the digest comparison is between two arms, so any nondeterminism masks it. Also: could two different arms collide? Only via hash collision. Nit.

  Another subtle one: noop_check: `a["digests"]["content"]` — KeyError if status ok but digests missing? digests set in reencode before status ok. OK. But `noop_check` runs even when... it iterates rec["cells"] = plan cells config: `for name, c in (rec.get("cells") or {}).items()` — c is the CELL CONFIG (kind/arm/args), and `ref = c.get("differs_from")`. OK.

  If dwv_default failed and dwv_pickim ok → flag True with why — intended.

  **Question 7: identity/provenance for cell arm.**

  cells_identity hashes: binary, FileFilter, lib/**, ldd deps of both binaries. NOT hashed:
  - `env.sh` itself! If env.sh changes (e.g. adds LD_LIBRARY_PATH pointing elsewhere, or exports OPENMS_DATA_PATH to a different tree) between launches, deps_sha256 might change (caught if lib paths change) but OPENMS_DATA_PATH change: share/OpenMS is NOT hashed at all! The tool reads 258 data files at run time (per prompt). PROVENANCE carries share_OpenMS_tree_sha256= but cell_provenance only checks sha256_published entries against binary/FileFilter/lib — it does NOT verify share_OpenMS_tree_sha256 against the actual tree. So a change in share/OpenMS (e.g., CV file, unimod, or the Bruker SDK configs) between launches changes results with no guard noticing. Wait — does anything check it? cell_provenance builds `want` from binary, FileFilter, lib — not share. PROVENANCE carries share_OpenMS_tree_sha256= but nothing compares it to a computed tree hash. And build_identity's cells entry: binary_sha256, lib_sha256, reencode_sha256, deps_sha256 — no share tree. So: G-V04/G-V11/G-V13 blind to share/OpenMS content. Given the tool reads those 258 files at runtime, this is a genuine gap. Severity: should-fix (would bite only if someone touches share/OpenMS between launches; on ceph, possible).

  - env.sh content not hashed; but cell_env re-sources it each launch and strips striped vars; if env.sh edited to add e.g. `-some default`? env.sh only sets env vars. It could export e.g. OMP_NUM_THREADS — wait, strip_var catches OMP_* presumably (STRIP_PREFIX includes OMP?). The selftest checks "cell env.sh exports an OpenMP knob" → refused. So env.sh changes that matter are caught by strip_var; but e.g. changing PATH or OPENMS_DATA_PATH target not caught except through deps. OPENMS_DATA_PATH: recorded in cells_meta["openms_data_path"] but table_guards only checks labels of cells_meta, not openms_data_path. So change OPENMS_DATA_PATH → label same, identity same → undetected. Same finding as share tree.

  - Also `arm_env`-style INHERIT_DROP handling: cell_env drops strip_var and INHERIT_DROP from base env — same.

  - PROVENANCE parsing: `if not line[:1].isspace(): block = ...` — lines not starting with whitespace reset block. Entries parsed `re.fullmatch(r"\s+([0-9a-f]{64})\s+(\S+)\s*", line)`. If PROVENANCE lists a file not in `want`, it's not checked (only `want` keys compared). E.g., provenance lists bin/diaWeaver with wrong hash but... no, want covers bin + FileFilter + lib. Files in arm outside those (share/, etc.) not covered. Same finding.

  - ldd_deps for cell: LIB_ROOTS check — fine.

  - env.sh is re-sourced at each cell_env call; but cell_env is called once per launch (in _node_run: `cenvs = {n: cell_env(q, n) ...}`) and identity computed once. check_identity compares ident0 built at start. Between launches, node_busy... fine.

  **Question 8: counting rules consistency.**

  `sage_by_charge` vs `sage_peptides`: 
  - sage_peptides: rank==1 AND peptide_q <= 0.01, distinct peptide set, target = label != "-1".
  - sage_by_charge: rows with rank==1 and q<=0.01; per peptide keep row with highest sage_discriminant_score; charge from that row; target = label != "-1".

  Differences:
  1. sage_peptides counts distinct peptides (set) — includes decoy in `peptides`. sage_by_charge: `peptides` = count of best entries per peptide — same set. target_peptides: sage_peptides adds to target set if label != "-1" for ANY qualifying row of that peptide... careful: in sage_peptides, for each qualifying row, adds peptide to decoy set if label=="-1" else target set. A peptide with both a decoy row and a target row ends in BOTH sets; target_peptides counts it as target, peptides counts once. In sage_by_charge, ONE row per peptide (best score) — if the best-scoring row is the decoy row, the peptide counts as decoy (not target) even if a target row exists. So target counts can differ between sage_peptides and sage_by_charge! add_split checks only: z1+z2p == all and all == r[k] for k in ("peptides","target_peptides") if k in by["all"]... `for k in [k for k in ("peptides", "target_peptides") if k in by["all"]]` — both keys exist in by. So it checks target_peptides too: by["all"]["target_peptides"] must equal r["target_peptides"]. Can they differ? sage_peptides target set: any row rank1 q<=0.01 with label != -1. sage_by_charge: target if the BEST row (by discriminant) among qualifying rows is target. Scenario: peptide X has decoy row (label -1) with score 0.9 and target row with score 0.8, both rank 1 q<=0.01. sage_peptides: X in both decoy and target sets → target_peptides includes X. sage_by_charge: best is decoy → not target. → mismatch → G-V19 abort. Fail-closed — good, aborts rather than wrong number. But could they differ WITHOUT tripping? by["all"]["target_peptides"] != r["target_peptides"] → die. So no silent wrong. But wait — could a peptide appear ONLY as decoy in the qualifying rows: sage_peptides target excludes; by_charge best decoy → excludes. Same. Only the both-rows case differs, caught. OK fail-closed.

     Also float parse: sage_peptides `float(row[iq])` may raise ValueError on bad row → crash (uncaught in run_search? caught by except Exception → CRASH record). sage_by_charge: `float(row[iq])` unguarded too, `float(row[isc])` unguarded, `int(row[iz])` unguarded → CRASH in add_split → caught by except Abort only! Look:

  ```python
  def add_split(...):
      try:
          by = (sage_by_charge if engine == "sage" else msf_by_charge)(Path(r["result_tsv"]))
          ...
      except Abort as a:
          r.update(status="failed", ...)
      write_json(p, r)
      return r["status"] == "ok"
  ```

     A ValueError in sage_by_charge escapes add_split → propagates into one_search → into unit() → uncaught → crashes phase 2 worker/queue?! one_search: `ok = run_search(...); return add_split(...) if ok and ext else ok`. ValueError from add_split propagates up through `unit`, through the search loop — the queue crashes. Record r written? add_split's write_json not reached on ValueError → record stays ok WITHOUT by_charge and WITHOUT cells hash in measure. Then next launch: search_done requires m.get("cells") == ctx cells → record's measure lacks cells → not done → re-run search. But the mzML was deleted? unit(): `if all(oks)` — the exception propagated before unlink. OK so mzML kept. But the queue crashed mid-phase-2 — a day lost, not a wrong number. Concrete trigger: a Sage TSV row with non-numeric charge? Sage writes integer charges. Or empty charge. Theoretical. Should-fix: catch Exception in add_split like Abort.

     Also rank: `row[ir] == "1"` same as sage_peptides. q: `float(row[iq]) <= 0.01` same. Charge: `int(row[iz])` — Sage charge column is integer. z1 stratum = charge 1; but diaWeaver never produces z=1 spectra... but charge here is the PSM's charge from the search, fine.

     Edge: same peptide best-score tie between two rows with different charges → first-seen wins (`sc > best` strict) → stratum depends on row order. Deterministic per file. Fine.

  `msf_by_charge` vs `msf_peptides`:
  - msf_peptides: `key = (peptide, modification_info or assigned_modifications)`; best hyperscore; then `sorted(best.values(), reverse=True)` — sorts TUPLES (hs, dec) descending: ties broken by dec (True > False → decoy first on tie!). Then t/d counting: `if t and d/t <= 0.01: acc = t` — acc = TARGET count; final peptides = acc (number of targets at deepest prefix).

    Note msf_peptides peptides = t (targets only!), i.e. "count targets" per rules.

  - msf_by_charge: key via `get(row, "peptide")` etc. — careful: msf_by_charge reads columns via header map `col`, with fallback `get` returning "" for missing. It requires only {"peptide","hyperscore","charge"} — "charge" REQUIRED (G-V19) whereas msf_peptides doesn't need charge. Fine.

    Sorting: `ranked = sorted(best.values(), key=lambda v: (v[0], v[1]), reverse=True)` — best.values() are (hs, dec, z); key (hs, dec) — matches msf_peptides' tuple sort (hs, dec) reverse — same ordering. Good.

    Prefix: `for i,(hs,dec,z): d, t = d+dec, t+(not dec); if t and d/t <= 0.01: cut = i+1`. msf_peptides: `acc = t` at deepest prefix where d/t<=0.01. msf_by_charge cut = i+1 = number of KEYS in the prefix (targets+decoys!), then counts `not dec` in ranked[:cut]. Are these equal? msf_peptides acc = t at that point = number of targets in prefix. msf_by_charge counts targets in ranked[:cut] = t. Equal. 

    Then "deepest prefix": msf_peptides keeps updating acc as long as condition holds — acc = t at the LAST i where d/t<=0.01. msf_by_charge cut = i+1 last time condition holds. Same i. Equal counts. Then stratum assignment uses the best PSM's charge per key. Split sums checked vs r["peptides"]: by["all"]["peptides"] must equal r["peptides"]. Equality holds by construction... unless int(get(row,"charge")) crashes (ValueError) → uncaught in add_split (only Abort caught) → crash as above.

    One difference: msf_peptides uses DictReader — rows with missing modification_info → row.get returns None → `or row.get("assigned_modifications", "")` → could be None → key contains None. msf_by_charge get() returns "" for missing. If TSV has NO modification_info and NO assigned_modifications columns: msf_peptides key = (pep, ""), msf_by_charge key = (pep, "" or "") = (pep,"") — wait `get(row,"modification_info") or get(row,"assigned_modifications")` → "" or "" → "". Same.

    Another: msf_by_charge `prots` split regex same. dec same. OK consistent.

  `entrap_strata` vs score(): score() picks best PSM per peptide by sage_discriminant_score among non-decoy rows (all(p.startswith("rev_")) skipped), ent = all proteins startswith tag or rev_tag. entrap_strata: same best-PSM choice (sc > best), prots split ";", skip if all rev_. Then writes joined line per peptide to stratum TSV and calls est.score on each stratum TSV. Consistency: score() on the stratum TSV re-parses the joined lines — the joined line is "\t".join(row) — original field text preserved, so re-parse identical. Stratum of best PSM charge — same rows → same peptide set per stratum. Partition checked. `out["all"]["target"] <= 0` → die. 

    But subtle: score() computes q per peptide from the BEST row's q? score(): best[pep] = (s, q, ent) — q from best-score row; then accepted = q <= q_cut presumably (the truncated source ends at best assignment; presumably accepted = peptides with q<=0.01). entrap_strata passes whole rows; score does its own q filter. Consistent between strata and all. The equality checks: accepted/target/entrap partition — checked. Good.

    BUT: entrap_strata picks best per peptide with `sc > best[...]` tie → first row wins; score() same tie behavior (`s > best[pep][0]`) — but order of rows in the stratum TSV is dict order of best.values() = insertion order = first-seen order, NOT original file order! If two PSMs for the same peptide tie on score with different charges... best row chosen during first pass over original order — same as score() on whole TSV (same original order). Then stratum TSVs contain one row per peptide — re-running score on them re-derives same best (only one row). Consistent.

    However: entrap_strata's `best` includes rows regardless of q; score() also builds best regardless of q then filters by q of best row. Same.

    Also `int(row[iz])` — ValueError if charge column has non-int → uncaught (entrap_strata called inside run_entrap's try which catches Exception → CRASH record, clean). OK.

    One more: `fin = lambda v: round(v,4) if v==v else None` — NaN CI → None. Fine.

    Potential mismatch: score() counts peptide as entrapment iff ALL proteins startswith tag or rev_tag. entrap_strata relies on score() for that. The stratum assignment uses the best PSM row's charge — score() on the stratum file re-parses proteins from that same line. Same.

  **Question 9: resume logic.**

  - `search_done` now requires `m.get("cells") == ctx["measure"].get("cells")`. run_search writes measure_sha256 with keys ("search","score") only: `"measure_sha256": {k: ctx["measure"].get(k) for k in ("search", "score")}` — run_search is UNCHANGED (in "unchanged functions" listing!). So a search record written by run_search lacks "cells" in measure → search_done false for extended plans → search never considered done → mzML never deleted?? Wait: add_split sets `r["measure_sha256"] = dict(r["measure_sha256"], cells=ctx["measure"]["cells"])` — so after add_split, the record gains cells hash → search_done true. Order in one_search: run_search then add_split (if ok and ext). So the intended flow: run_search ok → add_split adds cells hash + by_charge.

    Crash between run_search and add_split: record ok, measure lacks cells → search_done False → next launch re-runs the search (mzML still there since unit() unlink happens only when all oks... in the crashed launch, the exception—no crash here, just process died between). On resume, pending() true → run_search re-runs: `shutil.rmtree(sdir); sdir.mkdir` — re-run fine. No wrong number. But does the TABLE crash on a record without by_charge? render_cells_table: `bc = lambda g,z,k: fmt(S[t,g], lambda r: num(((r.get("by_charge") or {}).get(z) or {}).get(k)))` — None → num(None) → "-". OK no crash. But wait — the summary: `s_z2 = tot(lambda g: g["search"][t,"sage"]["by_charge"]["z2p"]["target_peptides"])` — full groups only; a group is "complete" only if all searches ok; a search record ok without cells hash... complete = `all(v[0] for v in S.values())` — S values are (r, None) if r["status"]=="ok" — regardless of by_charge! So a record ok-but-no-by_charge counts complete → summary KeyError 'by_charge'! When can that happen? If the queue crashed between run_search and add_split and the table is rendered before re-running. Records collected from ceph/scratch — render_cells_table → complete → `g["search"][t,"sage"]["by_charge"]` → KeyError crash. Severity: should-fix; realistic during the 26h run if anyone renders the table mid-run after a crash. Also G-V19-related: a record could be "adopted" for the table without its split. Answer to Q9: yes — table can crash (KeyError) on ok search records lacking by_charge, and nothing in render requires the cells hash on search records (table_guards requires one(measure_sha256) across oks — a record missing cells hash has DIFFERENT measure dict → "different harness code" abort actually! one(r["measure_sha256"] for r in oks) — dict {"search":x,"score":y} vs {"search":x,"score":y,"cells":z} → not equal → G-V13 die. So the table ABORTS rather than wrong number... but only if another ok record WITH cells exists. If ALL records lack cells (crash right after first search), one() trivially true (≤1 distinct) → proceeds → KeyError on by_charge in summary... wait summary requires full non-empty; complete requires pair ok and searches ok. If the only sample has all searches ok-without-cells → full → KeyError. But also `search(sid,t,g)` requires r["pair_run"] == p["pair_run"]. Yes. So KeyError. Also row-level: bc lambda handles missing by_charge gracefully ("-").

    Hmm wait, also `run_entrap` writes measure with cells included directly: `{k: ctx["measure"].get(k) for k in ("search","score","cells")}` — good.

  - Can a group be adopted with a failed or stale cell? ready_arms: `a.get("status","ok") != "ok" or not o ... → continue` — failed cell not in ready; pair_done requires ready == full tools list → group with failed cell not "done" → re-run whole. But searches of ok arms DO run (units from ready_arms). Then the group re-runs whole on next launch → new pair_run → old search records stale (pair_run mismatch) → re-searched. Wasteful but consistent. However: the re-run of the group runs the pair AND cells again — the ok arms' outputs overwritten. Fine.

    Stale cell adoption: ready_arms checks output file size/mtime. For a cell arm, output = reencoded.mzML; raw_output deleted (src.unlink()). If reencode succeeded, output path = reencoded. OK.

    Subtle: run_group writes rec BEFORE running (status running) and after. If runner SIGKILLed mid-group, record stays "running" → ready_arms: status != ok → None → re-run. OK.

    Another: `for old in (root/"runs"/s["id"]).glob("*/*/*.mzML"): old.unlink()` at group start — deletes previous attempts' mzMLs — including reencoded.mzML of a group whose searches are pending?! If the previous group was ok but a search failed, next launch: pair_done false (not all searches... wait pair_done doesn't check searches; ready_arms returns arms whose searches done OR file intact; pair_done requires ALL arms ready. If a search failed but mzML intact → arm ready → pair_done true → skip re-run, re-run search. Good. But if pair_done false for another reason (e.g. measure hash changed), run_group deletes ALL old mzMLs at start → searches of the new group fine. OK.

    But: crash between group ok and searches: on next launch pair_done → checks each arm: searches pending → verify file on disk (size+mtime). The reencoded file still there → ready. Good.

  - `search_done` for entrap records in `pending()` etc. — engines include "entrap"; rec_path(root, sid, tool, "entrap"). run_entrap writes to rec_path(root, s["id"], tool, "entrap") — consistent.

  - units built from ready_arms — but `pending(s, tool, e)` for e in engines — if a search record exists but stale pair_run → pending → re-run. Good.

  - One issue: `unit()` unlinks the mzML when all engines ok — for cell arms, `pairs[s["id"]]["arms"][tool]["output"]["path"]` = reencoded.mzML. Good. raw_output already deleted.

