ha256 logic and the MEASURED entries remain unchanged.I note that the scores for sage_peptides and msf_peptides remain unchanged. I plan to see if any input causes a different behavior for the plain plan, including the updated error message for tools and how node_busy handles empty cells. I'll also examine the busy_patterns logic more closely to confirm it behaves as expected.Vergleichslogik prüfen...The new code behaves the same for plain records: cells stay None, raw_sha256 and pair logic are unchanged, identity ignores a “cells” key for plain, ldd_deps’ default assignment is still equivalent, and the node/status helpers stay identical. The only change is that a plain plan with an unrecognized `entrapment` key no longer raises an error; it is now silently treated as plain.Ich plane zu prüfen, ob Schlüsselunterschiede weiterhin durchgelassen werden, dass `entrapment: false` jetzt stillabgewiesen wird, leere `cells:{}` abgelehnt wird und das als „nit“ klassifiziert wird; zusätzlich soll ich bestätigen, dass `search_done` und die glob‑Pattern in `run_pair` korrekt funktionieren und gleichzeitig potenzielle Fehler in der Zeilenbasierten `mzml_scan`‑Implementierung erkennen.He's checking that the hash routine strips IDs only from lines containing 'spectrumRef', especially when decoding is active, and noting how this influences the hash updates.Evaluating how lines containing “spectrumRef” trigger removal of ID attributes before hashing and determining which XML tags carry those IDs.Ich prüfe, ob der Regex `ids_re` die `id`, `index` und `spectrumRef` Attribute aus den Zeilen entfernt, um den Hash zu berechnen.I realize the `if b"spectrumRef" in line` check only applies to the first line, so `id=` attributes on other lines may be missed. I also question whether the digest truly normalizes two runs, given diaWeaver's scan renumbering and possible order differences, and what other variations might still persist.I observe that the `<cvParam ... MS:1000016 scan start time>` values and binary data remain identical. I’m exploring whether FileFilter’s re‑encoding, which sorts spectra by RT using `sortSpectra(true)` (potentially unstable for equal RTs), could affect determinism, though the RTs are likely distinct across windows. I’m also checking how the first occurrence of each PREC_CV is handled within a spectrum.He’s checking that the MS:1002815 (1/K0) CV term can appear both at scan level and in the selectedIon, and that OpenMS writes scanList before precursorList, so the scan‑level value would be seen first and the selectedIon one ignored. He notes that diaWeaver attaches it per spectrum but isn’t sure whether it’s emitted at scan or selectedIon level.When 1/K0 appears at both the scan level and in the selected ion, the digest takes the first occurrence (the scan‑level value), which may be semantically wrong. This follows OpenMS’s order but is a correctness nuance that should be addressed.Scan‑level data supersede, di Weaver’s 1/K0 may be per‑spectrum, mzml_scan correctly skips spectrumList entries, and numpress terms are counted globally.I’m noting that referenceableParamGroups are counted at the file level but diaWeaver likely ignores them, and that the second‐level Numpress term is also tallied and stripped by the FileFilter. I review how the parser accumulates cvParam accessions in a dictionary for each binaryDataArray, resetting appropriately and checking for Numpress flags. I also flag potential issues with long base64 binary data and the handling of <binaryDataArray> closing tags.Das nächste Glied prüft die Decodierung von CV-Werten, erkennt aber, dass ein zlib‑Error im mzml_scan‑Aufruf nicht abgefangen wird und somit das gesamte Zell-Group‑Processing zum Absturz bringt.A non‑Abort exception in a cell can halt the entire group, and decoding faults such as base64 errors, array length mismatches, or float conversion issues can similarly break execution; this is critical but unlikely unless the DIA forerunner output is malformed. A missing default array length leads to a minor skew in the median, while the digest construction using `repr(float(prec[k]))` remains stable across identical spectra.I’m reviewing the prec_digest logic, noting how valueless cvParams are handled, how float values are normalized for hashing, confirming that sorting and ID stripping yield identical blocks across runs, examining multilevel tags, and considering collision possibilities between different processing arms.Same‑arm runs produce identical content digests because FileFilter re‑sorts by RT but preserves ids that are subsequently stripped, and the digest sorts blocks so any ordering instability is neutralized.I’m evaluating whether nondeterminism in diaWeaver’s peak picking could make two runs produce different binary content, even though G‑V18 flags when distinct cells share identical digests. If the –bruker:ms1_centroid arguments are truly no‑ops, identical outputs will trigger the flag, but I’m still unsure if repeated runs of the same arm could actually diverge in digest.Ich überlege, dass ein Wa‑Inhalt die Flag‑Auslöschung nicht falsifiziert, weil die Prüfziffern der beiden Zellen im selben Lauf unterschiedlich sein könnten. Weiterhin prüfe ich die Regex‑Entfernung der `id=`‑Attribute, ob sie nur in der ersten Zeile gilt, und ob dadurch ein leichtes Hash‑Problem entsteht. Dann denke ich darüber nach, ob die bereits vorhandene Digest‑Logik tatsächlich alle relevanten Zeilen normalisiert und vergleichbare Ergebnisse liefert.I realized G‑V17 only checks the precursor digest, so changes to peak data would go undetected, and I’ll also flag spectra that lack an m/z array.Examining how missing decode flags, truncated arrays, and mislabelled compression affect validation and noting that numpress terms in global groups can trigger false negatives.Inspecting post‑check numpress, regex whitespace, float parsing, and spectrum start conditions.I’m checking whether a `<spectrum>` tag ever splits across lines, noting that `<binary>` data can be very long, and considering that different fragment‐trace IDs may be assigned in separate runs, which could change the hash of the mzML scan blocks.I’m reviewing the subprocess monitoring loop that checks elapsed time and memory against limits and kills the process if exceeded, noting the poll‑interval granularity and the incomplete kill_session call.Assessing that killpg will terminate all in the session—including diaWeaver—and checking that the memory‑floor heuristic may be bypassed for certain pair runs.I notice that `run_capped` only watches the cell’s own process, but a cell can still be killed if other tenants bring down MemAvailable; the guard then aborts instead of retrying. I also observe that in `run_cell` the stopped flag is examined before calling `parse_time_log` or `check_exit`, which prevents processing of corrupted timing data from a killed job.Considering how GNU time reports a SIGKILL exit status and the possibility of timing conflicts when the cap triggers right as a tool finishes, I’ll need to handle a potential ProcessLookupError during the session kill.Accounting for a race where the tool finishes just before the kill triggers, causing a ProcessLookupError but resulting in a “killed: wall cap” status.Verifying that a new time session starts correctly and reviewing how SIGTERM and SIGKILL impact process termination and cleanup.Gate waits 20 min, flags the run as contended, and an orphaned diaWeaver can still execute concurrently, causing CPU contention that the monitor records.I’m checking the busy‑patterns logic: an orphan process with “diaWeaver -in” is considered busy, so the gate waits 20 minutes before proceeding, and the run is marked as contended—this is intended. I note that `proc_snapshot()` will skip the memory test if /proc is unreadable, which is acceptable, and that the monitor loop sleeps with `p.wait(timeout=MON_S)` while calling `proc_snapshot()` every cycle. I’ll next look into the `run_…` routine.Sie prüft, ob Nicht‑Abort‑Ausnahmen in einer Zelle die gesamte Gruppe zum Absturz bringen und damit gegen die vorgesehenen Semantik verstoßen.Examining a truncated mzML that throws a ValueError in G‑V17, causing the entire group to crash before later steps.Considering numeric conversion and zlib decoding errors.Exploring how reference arm outcomes affect `pair_done` and noting that diaWeaver’s fragment trace IDs can differ between runs, potentially altering the no‑op digest.V18 compares dwv_default versus dwv_pickim within the same run; a genuine no‑op will be ignored if diaWeaver is nondeterministic, letting differing outputs slip through. The self‑test only covers a deterministic fake, so a real nondeterministic no‑op can go unflagged.Because digest comparison is only between the two arms, nondeterminism in the output can hide a genuine difference, resulting in a false negative.  
